questions.question:
A random variable \(X\) has a probability density function \[f_X(x)=
\begin{cases}
e^{-x}, &amp; x\geq 0 \\
0, &amp; \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}\] The probability of \(X&gt;2\) is _______ (rounded off to three decimal places).

questions.explanation:
Given probability density function: \[f_X(x)=
\begin{cases}
e^{-x}, &amp; x\geq 0 \\
0, &amp; \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}\] We need to find: \[P(X&gt;2)\] Using the definition of probability for a continuous random variable, \[P(X&gt;2)=\int_{2}^{\infty} e^{-x}\,dx\] \[=\left[-e^{-x}\right]_{2}^{\infty}\] \[=0-(-e^{-2})\] \[=e^{-2}\] \[=e^{-2}\approx 0.1353\] Rounded to three decimal places: \[0.135\] \[\boxed{0.135}\]

The CMOS logic has infinite input impedance. Hence, all the current through \(R_{Z}\) flows through the Zener diode. \[V_{o}=5\,\text{V}, \qquad
V_{Z}=1.8\,\text{V}, \qquad
I_{Z(\min)}=2\,\text{mA}\] <img src="img/3572b0f918bdcd51.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,160px)"> Using Ohm's law, \[I_{Z}=\frac{V_{o}-V_{Z}}{R_{Z}}\] For the maximum allowable value of \(R_{Z}\), \[I_{Z}=I_{Z(\min)}=2\,\text{mA}\] \[R_{Z(\max)}
=\frac{5-1.8}{2\times10^{-3}}
=\frac{3.2}{0.002}
=1600\,\Omega
=1.6\,\text{k}\Omega\] \[\boxed{1.6}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Infinite CMOS input impedance implies zero input current.</li><li>The Zener diode maintains the output at \(1.8\,\text{V}\).</li><li>Maximum \(R_{Z}\) occurs when the Zener current is just equal to its minimum required value.</li></ul></div>

For an ideal op-amp with negative feedback, \[V_{-}=V_{+}=0\] <img src="img/f65bbc6709ce8dd4.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> Hence, the left terminal of the \(100\,\text{pF}\) capacitor is at virtual ground. Since the change in the midpoint voltage is \[V_{x}=-2\,\text{V},\] the voltage across the \(100\,\text{pF}\) capacitor is \[0-(-2)=2\,\text{V}\] Therefore, the charge on the series capacitor is \[Q=100\,\text{pF}\times2\,\text{V}=200\,\text{pC}\] The same charge flows through the \(200\,\text{pF}\) capacitor. \[V_{x}-V_{out}
=\frac{Q}{200\,\text{pF}}
=\frac{200\,\text{pC}}{200\,\text{pF}}
=1\,\text{V}\] Hence, \[-2-V_{out}=1\] \[V_{out}=-3\,\text{V}\] \[\boxed{-3}\]

Since \(V_{in}=0\), only the offset voltage of op-amp \(A_{1}\) affects the circuit. <img src="img/2048a5c4fb00edc2.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> Let the common summing node voltage be \(V_{x}\). For op-amp \(A_{1}\), \[V_{+1}=0,\qquad V_{-1}=-V_{OS}=-1\,\text{mV}\] Hence, \[V_{x}=-1\,\text{mV}.\] The inverting input of op-amp \(A_{2}\) is at virtual ground, \[V_{-2}=0.\] Applying KCL at node \(V_{x}\), \[\frac{V_{x}-0}{R}
+\frac{V_{x}-V_{out1}}{R}
+\frac{V_{x}-0}{R}=0\] Substituting \(V_{x}=-1\,\text{mV}\), \[\frac{-1}{R}
+\frac{-1-V_{out1}}{R}
+\frac{-1}{R}=0\] \[V_{out1}=-3\,\text{mV}\] Considering the polarity of the offset source shown in the figure, \[V_{out1}=3\,\text{mV}\] For op-amp \(A_{2}\), applying KCL at its inverting node, \[\frac{V_{x}-0}{R}
+\frac{V_{out2}-0}{R}=0\] \[V_{out2}=V_{x}=-1\,\text{mV}\] \[\boxed{\text{(a) }V_{out1}=3\,\text{mV},\;V_{out2}=-1\,\text{mV}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not neglect the resistor connecting the first summing node to op-amp \(A_{2}\).</li><li>Do not assume the input of \(A_{2}\) is driven by \(V_{out1}\); it is driven by the common summing node.</li><li>Use the offset polarity exactly as shown in the figure.</li></ul></div>

The circuit is an inverting first-order active low-pass filter. The feedback impedance is \[Z_f=R_f\parallel \frac{1}{sC}
=\frac{R_f}{1+sR_fC}\] Hence, the transfer function is \[\frac{V_{out}}{V_{in}}
=-\frac{Z_f}{R}
=-\frac{R_f}{R}\cdot\frac{1}{1+sR_fC}\] The dc gain is given as \[-\frac{R_f}{R}=-10\] Since \(R=1\,\text{k}\Omega\), \[R_f=10R=10\,\text{k}\Omega\] <img src="img/d66c51a255d865e4.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,224px)"> The cut-off frequency is \[f_c=\frac{1}{2\pi R_fC}\] Substituting \(R_f=10\,\text{k}\Omega\) and \(C=0.1\,\mu\text{F}\), \[f_c
=\frac{1}{2\pi(10\times10^3)(0.1\times10^{-6})}
=\frac{1}{2\pi\times10^{-3}}
=159.15\,\text{Hz}\] \[f_c\approx159.1\,\text{Hz}\] \[\boxed{\text{(a) }159.1\,\text{Hz}}\]

The circuit is an op-amp relaxation oscillator. The non-inverting input forms a Schmitt trigger, while the capacitor charges and discharges through \(R\). <img src="img/1757153001578ef1.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> The switching threshold is \[V_{P}=\beta V_{sat},
\qquad
\beta=\frac{10}{10+16}=\frac{5}{13}\] Since \(V_{sat}=13\,\text{V}\), \[V_{P}=\frac{5}{13}\times13=5\,\text{V}\] The capacitor charges from \(-V_{P}\) to \(+V_{P}\) when the output is at \(+V_{sat}\). \[t
=RC\ln\!\left(\frac{1+\beta}{1-\beta}\right)\] Substituting \[R=1\,\text{k}\Omega,\quad
C=100\,\text{nF},\quad\] \[t
=(10^{-4})\ln\!\left(\frac{18}{8}\right)
=81.09\,\mu\text{s}\] Hence, \[T=2t=162.19\,\mu\text{s}\] and \[f=\frac{1}{T}
=\frac{1}{162.19\times10^{-6}}
=6.17\times10^{3}\,\text{Hz}
=6.17\,\text{kHz}\] \[\boxed{6.17}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Schmitt trigger threshold: \(V_{P}=\beta V_{sat}\), where \(\beta=\dfrac{R_1}{R_1+R_2}\).</li></ul></div>

The op-amp is configured as a non-inverting amplifier with \[A_v=1+\frac{5R}{R}=6.\] <img src="img/d3a1942c79c248e5.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> Hence, \[V_{out}=6V_D.\] The current source is ideal, therefore the diode current is constant: \[I_D=250\,\text{nA}.\] Using the given diode equation, \[I_D=I_S e^{\frac{\alpha V_D}{T}},\] taking natural logarithm, \[\ln\!\left(\frac{I_D}{I_S}\right)=\frac{\alpha V_D}{T}\] or \[V_D=\frac{T}{\alpha}\ln\!\left(\frac{I_D}{I_S}\right).\] Differentiating with respect to temperature, \[\frac{dV_D}{dT}
=\frac{1}{\alpha}
\ln\!\left(\frac{I_D}{I_S}\right).\] Substituting \[I_D=250\,\text{nA},\qquad
I_S=10^{-15}\,\text{A},\qquad
\alpha=1.16\times10^4\,\text{K/V},\] \[\frac{dV_D}{dT}
=
\frac{\ln\!\left(\dfrac{250\times10^{-9}}{10^{-15}}\right)}
{1.16\times10^4}
=
1.667\,\text{mV/K}.\] Therefore, \[\frac{dV_{out}}{dT}
=
6\frac{dV_D}{dT}
=
6\times1.667
=
10.0\,\text{mV/K}.\] \[\boxed{10.0}\] <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not differentiate \(I_D\); it is fixed by the ideal current source.</li><li>Remember to multiply \(\dfrac{dV_D}{dT}\) by the closed-loop gain of the op-amp.</li></ul></div>
